fact WorkItem(id: "WI-633", description: some(value: "Resolver: nonlinear rule-head match completeness — repeated head vars impose STRUCTURAL identity, not unification, on doubly-concrete occurrences, and the inverse orientation leaks an unbound output. Found by the WI-624 /code-review verify pass (both empirically reproduced). (1) DOUBLY-CONCRETE: head p(box(v: ?v), ?v) queried p(box(v: some(?x)), some(42)) — true semantics binds ?x=42 (unify the two ?v matches); today the discrim var edges double-bind the synthetic MAX-n vid (discrim.rs:816 as_bind_value), bind_term's re-bind check is views_structurally_equal (structural, no unification), the mismatch flags is_contradiction and resolve.rs drops the candidate: SILENT 0 solutions. This is why stdlib Map.binds rule 1 must keep its LINEAR head + explicit '?v <=> ?v2' body unify (see the map.anthill NB comment; the WI-624 session briefly reverted it to nonlinear and the verify pass caught the false failure). (2) INVERSE ORIENTATION: query var buried in the compound occurrence with a concrete term at the bare occurrence — binds(put(empty,1,?y), 1, 42) / unbox0(box(v: ?out2), 42) — yields a DEFINITE solution with the output var left silently UNBOUND (the WI-624 leak shape through the route the fix pair does not cover; WI-624 fixed the forward orientation: concrete in compound, flex at bare). Ignored repro tests: wi616_semantic_eq_test::{nonlinear_head_doubly_concrete_unifies, nonlinear_head_inverse_orientation_binds}. Direction: either thread rule-var double-binds through real unification at the leaf/with_fresh_vars (turning the structural-conflict drop into a unify attempt and threading inverse links through the synthetic-entry direction), or gate/lint nonlinear heads at load. NB: whichever lands must keep WI-624's occurs check (with_fresh_vars pass 2 -> answer_links.contradiction) intact."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-07-05T07:38:00Z"))

